 {
  "id": "evt_9767f8046b914bb1b3d452b81c9db8fb",
  "kind": "plan",
  "source": "agent",
  "timestamp": "2026-06-11T04:43:23.109158Z",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "seq": 8,
  "meta": {},
  "summary": "Build a sensor dashboard with a Python API (port 3000) serving CSV as JSON, and a Vite+React frontend (port 8000) showing a data table and summary card.",
  "steps": [
   {
    "title": "Create Python API server",
    "detail": "Write a lightweight Python script (using built-in http.server + csv module, no pip installs needed) that reads uploads/sensor_readings.csv and serves it as JSON at GET /api/readings on port 3000."
   },
   {
    "title": "Scaffold Vite + React frontend",
    "detail": "Run `npm create vite@latest frontend -- --template react` in the workspace, install dependencies, and configure the dev server to run on port 8000 with a proxy to port 3000 for /api requests."
   },
   {
    "title": "Build React dashboard components",
    "detail": "Create an App component that fetches /api/readings, renders a summary card (count, min temp, max temp), and displays all readings in a styled table with columns: timestamp, sensor, temperature."
   },
   {
    "title": "Start both servers and verify in browser",
    "detail": "Launch the Python API on port 3000, then start the Vite dev server on port 8000. Confirm the page loads, the table populates with data, and the summary card shows correct count/min/max values."
   }
  ],
  "revision": 1,
  "context": "## What I found\n\n- **CSV data** (`uploads/sensor_readings.csv`): 200 rows, 3 columns \u2014 `timestamp`, `sensor`, `temperature_c`. Sensors include nvme, amdgpu, k10temp, spd5118. Temperatures range roughly 32\u201362\u00b0C.\n- **Workspace**: Empty aside from the uploaded CSV \u2014 fresh start.\n\n## Approach\n\n- **Backend**: Pure Python using `http.server` and `csv` \u2014 zero external dependencies. Parses the CSV once at startup and returns JSON on `/api/readings`.\n- **Frontend**: Vite + React (the standard modern stack). Vite's dev proxy will forward `/api/*` to `localhost:3000`, so the frontend just calls `/api/readings` as if it were same-origin.\n- **Summary card**: Computes `count` (total readings), `min` and `max` of `temperature_c` across all data.\n- **Table**: Shows all 200 rows with timestamp, sensor name, and temperature.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\n- No database or caching \u2014 the CSV is read once at startup into memory. Fine for 200 rows.\n- Using Python's stdlib instead of Flask/FastAPI avoids a pip install and keeps things simple.\n- Vite proxy means the frontend code doesn't need to know about port 3000."
 },
